INSERT INTO canonical_records (record_id,source_file,source_page,domain,stem,options_json,source_answer,source_explanation,provenance_json,visual_asset,visual_url,options_mode,current_answer,current_answer_status,current_evidence_json,currentness_risk,record_json,active_quiz,import_version,imported_at) VALUES ('de8961c45b52b4ba946f','AMEDEX 2025 Endo_ breast 1-50.pdf','66','Endocrinology/Breast','Adrian is a 17-year-old boy who has presented to your GP clinic with painful breast enlargement for the past three months, as shown in the accompanying photograph. He has asthma controlled with inhaled salbutamol and beclomethasone, otherwise takes no medications, plays football on his school team, and denies illicit drug use. Examination shows unilateral right breast enlargement starting from the nipple and spreading concentrically, with mild tenderness, no discharge or lymphadenopathy, and an unremarkable testicular examination. Which one of the following is the most likely cause of this presentation?','{"A":"Inhaled salbutamol.","B":"Inhaled beclomethasone.","C":"Misuse of anabolic steroids.","D":"Physiologic gynecomastia of adolescents.","E":"A testicular tumor."}','C','Option C Is correct

The photograph and the description of physical
findings favor gynecomastia as the most likely
diagnosis.

The three major causes of gynecomastia are
physiologic, idiopathic, and drug/substance abuse
related. Physiologic gynecomastia comprises 25%
of all cases and is seen in neonatal, pubertal, and
males between 50 and 80 years of age.
Medications, including drugs of abuse such as
alcohol, anabolic steroids, amphetamine, and
Opiates, account for 10 to 25% of cases. In 25% of
cases, gynecomastia is idiopathic, and no
identifiable cause is found. Other less likely causes
are primary and secondary hypogonadism, chronic
liver disease, chronic renal disease, tumors (e.g.,
testicular, adrenal), malnutrition/ starvation, and

hyperthyroidism, as well as other rarer causes.

In this boy, persistent pubertal gynecomastia (not
an option) can be the best option, given the
absence of other physical findings that may
suggest a medical condition such as
hypogonadism or testicular tumors. Persistent
pubertal gynecomastia is classified under
idiopathic gynecomastia and is defined as pubertal
gynecomastia not resolving after two years or
persisting beyond 17 years of age.

NOTE—By the age of 17, pubertal
avnecomasti*@mrocovco trouve OF DOVS.

NOTE—By the age of 17, pubertal
gynecomastia is resolved in 90% of boys.

Gynecomastia beyond this age is no
longer termed physiologic and is categorized
under idiopathic pathologic gynecomastia.

In the absence of idiopathic gynecomastia, which
Statistically seems to be the likely explanation,
medications and specific substances should be
considered as a possibility. Many medications,
based either on anecdotal reports or studies of
different quality, are known to cause gynecomastia.
Of Adrian''s medications, neither inhaled
salbutamol (option A) nor inhaled betamethasone
(option B) can cause gynecomastia. Another
possibility to consider, especially in younger
patients with athletic behavior, is the misuse of
anabolic steroids. This boy is a football player and
is likely to have used or is using anabolic steroids
for enhanced performance. In younger patients
with gynecomastia who also have athletic
behaviors, misuse of anabolic steroids should
always be considered as a possibility. In fact, it is
the best option, among others, in this scenario.

(Option D) Physiologic gynecomastia of
adolescence is expected to resolve between

six months and two years of onset and usually
does not persist beyond 17 years of age. On the
other hand, the breast enlargement in the

photograph exceeds 5cm in diameter

‘macromastia). Macromastia suggests patholo

rather than physiologic gynecomastia.
