INSERT INTO canonical_records (record_id,source_file,source_page,domain,stem,options_json,source_answer,source_explanation,provenance_json,visual_asset,visual_url,options_mode,current_answer,current_answer_status,current_evidence_json,currentness_risk,record_json,active_quiz,import_version,imported_at) VALUES ('8a1bc2e2d888001b210c','AMEDEX 2025 Endo_ breast 1-50.pdf','130','Endocrinology/Breast','All of the following options are features of diabetic autonomic neuropathy EXCEPT:','{"A":"Bradycardia.","B":"Urinary retention.","C":"Sexual impotence.","D":"Diarrhea.","E":"Foot ulcers."}','E','Option E Is correct

Autonomic neuropathy is one of the
complications of diabetes mellitus. Diabetic
autonomic neuropathy is a common and often
debilitating condition. Clinical manifestations of
diabetic autonomic neuropathy include the
following:

e Skin - dry skin (due to lack of sweating) or
excessive sweating in defined areas

e Eye - poor dark adaptation, sensitivity to
bright lights

¢ Cardiovascular - postural ligntheadedness,
fainting, orthostatic tachycardia,
orthostatic bradycardia and orthostatic
hypotension

e Urinary - urgency, incontinence, dribbling,
and bladder emptying abnormalities

¢ Gastrointestinal - diarrhea, constipation,
nausea and vomiting

e Sexual - erectile dysfunction and
ejaculatory failure in men, loss of ability to
reach sexual climax in women

Although foot ulcers, a major source of
morbidity in patients with diabetes, are a feature
of diabetic neuropathy, it is unrelated to
autonomic dysfunction. Diabetic foot ulcers

have a different pathophysiology, mostly
including peripheral neuropathy and ische

from peripheral vascular disease. Poor

racictanca tn <p mere ic lees aA cCNANTtrihi iting

e Sexual - erectile dysfunction and

ejaculatory failure in men, loss of ability to
reach sexual climax in women

Although foot ulcers, a major source of
morbidity in patients with diabetes, are a feature
of diabetic neuropathy, it is unrelated to
autonomic dysfunction. Diabetic foot ulcers

have a different pathophysiology, mostly
including peripheral neuropathy and ischemia
from peripheral vascular disease. Poor
resistance to infections is also a contributing
factor.

Reference(s) ° UpToDate - Diabetic autonomic
neuropathy

* RACGP - General practice manag
ement of type 2 diabetes - Neurop
athy

¢ PubMed - Cardiac autonomic ne
uropathy: Risk factors, diagnosis a
nd treatment

